The array is sorted by recursion on its length. First, the function sorts the first `n - 1` values. Then it takes the last value and moves larger values one place to the right until the correct position is open.

For example, with `[4, 2, 3]`, the first call sorts `[4, 2]` into `[2, 4]`. The value `3` is then placed between them. The input vector is changed directly.

```cpp
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
private:
    void sortPrefix(vector<int>& nums, int length) {
        if (length <= 1) {
            return;
        }

        sortPrefix(nums, length - 1);

        int value = nums[length - 1];
        int position = length - 2;

        while (position >= 0 && nums[position] > value) {
            nums[position + 1] = nums[position];
            position--;
        }

        nums[position + 1] = value;
    }

public:
    vector<int> insertionSort(vector<int>& nums) {
        sortPrefix(nums, static_cast<int>(nums.size()));
        return nums;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case. Space complexity: `O(n)` for the recursive call stack, with `O(1)` extra array space.

The helper treats the first `length - 1` elements as an already sorted part. After that recursive call finishes, the last element is inserted by shifting bigger elements to the right.

The important edge cases are handled by the base condition: an empty array and an array with one value need no work. Negative numbers and duplicates also work because comparisons use only `>`.

