This one keeps a separate sorted slice. The recursive function takes the next input value, inserts it into the right place in the sorted slice, and then processes the rest. It is less space-friendly than changing the input slice, but the insertion step is quite direct.

Dry run with `[2, 1, 3]`: start with `[2]`, insert `1` at index `0` to get `[1, 2]`, then append `3` to get `[1, 2, 3]`.

```go
package solution

func insertionSort(nums []int) []int {
    sorted := make([]int, 0, len(nums))

    var addNext func(int)
    addNext = func(index int) {
        if index == len(nums) {
            return
        }

        value := nums[index]
        insertAt := 0
        for insertAt < len(sorted) && sorted[insertAt] <= value {
            insertAt++
        }

        sorted = append(sorted, 0)
        copy(sorted[insertAt+1:], sorted[insertAt:])
        sorted[insertAt] = value

        addNext(index + 1)
    }

    addNext(0)
    return sorted
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because inserting into the slice can shift many values. Space complexity: `O(n)` for the sorted slice and `O(n)` for recursion. The original slice is not changed.

This version moves each item left by swapping it with the previous item. The recursive call continues with the item at the next index, and another recursive call walks the current item backward while it is smaller.

A short dry run for `[3, 1, 2]` is: `1` swaps with `3`, giving `[1, 3, 2]`; then `2` swaps with `3`, producing `[1, 2, 3]`. It is easy to follow, but swaps do more assignments than shifting a saved value.

```java
class Solution {
    private void moveLeft(int[] nums, int index) {
        if (index == 0 || nums[index - 1] <= nums[index]) {
            return;
        }

        int temporary = nums[index];
        nums[index] = nums[index - 1];
        nums[index - 1] = temporary;

        moveLeft(nums, index - 1);
    }

    private void sortFrom(int[] nums, int index) {
        if (index >= nums.length) {
            return;
        }

        moveLeft(nums, index);
        sortFrom(nums, index + 1);
    }

    public int[] insertionSort(int[] nums) {
        sortFrom(nums, 1);
        return nums;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case and `O(n)` when the array is already sorted. Space complexity: `O(n)` because of recursion. Duplicate values stay in their original relative order.

